    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Founded in 1902, Fletcher was named for Fletcher Dodge, who gave half of his 160-acre homestead for the new townsite.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=FL001"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Development in Fletcher was spurred by the Oklahoma City and Western Railroad crossing the homestead of Fletcher Dodge.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=FL001"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Throughout its history, Fletcher has largely been an agricultural community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=FL001"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "By 1936, Fletcher had three cotton gins, an elevator, two chicken hatcheries, and a large sweet potato house.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=FL001"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The community of Fletcher also featured three cream and poultry stations by 1936.",
